Read the trust downward: mode 1 trusts the client's own recency of state;
mode 2 trusts slot arithmetic plus key-provisioning discipline; mode 3
trusts roster coordination plus physical destruction.

Accept a certified chain if and only if:
\begin{enumerate}
\item it \emph{validates}: the five checks of Section~\ref{sec:rules}
  --- certificate, signatures at declared versions, linkage, density,
  and the key-version discipline with the deployment's pin
  (\code{validCertifiedChain} plus the mode's key checks);
\item it is \emph{recent}: $\mathit{now} \le \mathit{tip.slot} + b$ on the
  verifier's own clock, where real time is at most $\delta$ slots ahead of
  $\mathit{now}$ ($R \le \mathit{now} + \delta$) and $b + \delta \le n$.

\begin{theorem}[Recent chains share their deep blocks; Lean
\code{timed\_light\_client\_safety}]\label{thm:lc}
Fix $n \ge 1$ and a deployment genesis $G$. Suppose:
\begin{itemize}
\item a timed execution (\code{TimedExecution}): signing happens at real slots;
  at a bad real slot the producer's key signs arbitrarily many blocks with
  any stamp in its residue class (\code{key\_match}); an honest real slot
  signs only its own current block (\code{honest\_stamp}) and at most once
  (\code{honest\_once}); a block is signed only once its parent is available
  (\code{chain\_order}); and collision resistance holds over occurring blocks
  (\code{id\_inj});
\item the fault budget over real slots: $\code{ByzantineBounded}\ n\ \mathit{bad}$;
\item the verifier is at real slot $R$, and every producer signature it accepts
  was produced at some real slot no later than $R$ (\code{hbridge}: EUF-CMA
  plus causality);
\item there are two presentations, each consisting of a grounded certificate
  claim (\code{GroundedCert}) and a suffix that passes the validator:
  linkage to the claim (\code{hLink}), internal linkage (\code{hLinks}),
  density of maturing windows (\code{hDense}), and verifying signatures
  (\code{hSigned});
\item both tips are recent in real time: $R \le \mathit{tip.slot} + n$.
\end{itemize}
Then, taking any histories the two groundings attest (\code{GroundedHistory}),
the two full chains (attested history concatenated with suffix) agree at
every height $h$ with $h + n$ below both full lengths. Equivalently, they
agree on everything at least $n$ blocks below the lower tip.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The client sync rule; Lean \code{sync\_rule\_timed},
\code{sync\_rule\_mem\_timed}]\label{thm:refresh}
Fix $n \ge 1$ and a deployment genesis $G$. Suppose:
\begin{itemize}
\item the key-stealing signature surface (\code{hEUF}): \code{KeyStealingEUFCMA} with adversary keys $\mathit{Stolen} := \code{stolenOf}\ \mathit{stolenAt}$;
\item hash injectivity (\code{hHash}): $\code{SignedHashInjective}\ (\code{KeyStealingSigned}\ n\ \mathit{ops}\ \mathit{registry})\ G$;
\item cadence and anchor (\code{hVPrev} \dots\ \code{hCadence}, \code{hA}, \code{hA'}): the client re-verified a valid chain $\mathit{sc}_{\mathrm{prev}}$ longer than $n$ blocks at time $t$ when its tip was recent ($t \le \mathit{tipPrev.slot} + n$), took as anchor $A$ the block $n$ deep below $\mathit{tipPrev}$ (\code{hAnchor}), returns within $n$ slots of that sync ($\mathit{now} \le t + n$, \code{hCadence}), and both currently presented chains $\mathit{sc}, \mathit{sc}'$ contain $A$;
\item rent rate (\code{hRent}): for every $n$-slot window $u$ starting in the trailing $5n$ slots or later ($\mathit{now} < u + 5n$), rented slots (\code{badSlotsIn}) total at most $\rho$;
\item theft census (\code{hTheft}): for every $n$-slot window $u$ starting in the trailing $5n$ slots or later ($\mathit{now} < u + 5n$), producers hit by a theft at any real slot $r$ with $u < r + d$ (\code{recentTheftProducersK}) total at most $T$;
\item the fault budget (\code{hRT}): $\rho + T \le \fmax$ on windows starting in the trailing $5n$ slots or later;
\item the reaction delay $d$ (\code{hReacts}): within $d$ slots of a theft of seat $i$'s version $j$, the version in force for $i$ on the chain being validated is above $j$ (\code{Reacts});
\item validity (\code{hVal}, \code{hVal'}): both $\mathit{sc}$ and $\mathit{sc}'$ pass the mode-1 validator (\code{validSignedChainK$'$});
\item recency (\code{hRecent}, \code{hRecent'}): both tips are recent ($\mathit{now} \le \mathit{sTip.slot} + \Delta$);
\item chain length (\code{hLong}): both chains are longer than $n$ blocks;
\item tip height: the tips are at equal height for the equal-height form (\code{hTipHeight}: $\mathit{sTip.height} = \mathit{sTip'.height}$), or the lower tip height is at most the other's for the membership form (\code{hLe}: $\mathit{sTip.height} \le \mathit{sTip'.height}$).
\end{itemize}
Then any two accepted, recent chains longer than $n$ blocks that both contain the anchor agree: at equal tip heights, on the block $n$ below each tip ($B = B'$); at unequal heights, the lower chain's $n$-deep block is a block of the taller chain, at least $n$ deep there.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Untimed residue, derived; Lean
\code{sigUnforgeableRecent\_of\_timed}]\label{thm:timed-uniq}
In any timed execution satisfying the EUF-CMA bridge and no back-dating,
the untimed residue \code{SigUnforgeableRecent} holds for the projected log
--- at every recency window, the bar being a parameter the proof never uses.
\end{theorem}
